  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(5x)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{5},\\,x=a+\\frac{2\\pi n}{5},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5x)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n5x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,5x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n, & \\,5x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{5}, & \\,x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{30}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{7\\pi }{30}.$",
